      {"type":"mc","q":"True or false: Multiple local sources can build a more complete account than one source alone.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explain":"True. Multiple local sources can build a more complete account than one source alone. The lesson evidence supports this claim.","audio":"Evaluate this claim using the evidence from Communities."},
      {"type":"mc","q":"An early settlement map clusters homes near a river crossing and fertile land. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["The location reflects opportunities provided by the environment and transportation.","Citizens contribute only when they hold elected office.","Local decisions and economic changes never affect population.","One source should replace all other evidence about a community."],"answer":"The location reflects opportunities provided by the environment and transportation.","explain":"The location reflects opportunities provided by the environment and transportation. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"An early settlement map clusters homes near a river crossing and fertile land. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["Local decisions and economic changes never affect population.","One source should replace all other evidence about a community.","Communities form without any relationship to people or place.","The directory reveals institutions serving different educational, economic, civic, and cultural needs."],"answer":"The directory reveals institutions serving different educational, economic, civic, and cultural needs.","explain":"The directory reveals institutions serving different educational, economic, civic, and cultural needs. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Volunteer records show residents organizing food distribution after a flood. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["Communities form without any relationship to people or place.","A community is defined only by the number of houses it contains.","The records document civic action responding to a shared community need.","One source should replace all other evidence about a community."],"answer":"The records document civic action responding to a shared community need.","explain":"The records document civic action responding to a shared community need. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"Volunteer records show residents organizing food distribution after a flood. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Census and street maps show population growth after a factory and rail stop opened. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["Citizens contribute only when they hold elected office.","Together the sources suggest jobs and transportation helped attract new residents.","Communities form without any relationship to people or place.","A community is defined only by the number of houses it contains."],"answer":"Together the sources suggest jobs and transportation helped attract new residents.","explain":"Together the sources suggest jobs and transportation helped attract new residents. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"Census and street maps show population growth after a factory and rail stop opened. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"A map shows neighborhood growth, a newspaper explains a new road, and interviews describe residents' experiences. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?","choices":["The sources corroborate growth while contributing different kinds of evidence about its causes and effects.","A community is defined only by the number of houses it contains.","Citizens contribute only when they hold elected office.","Local decisions and economic changes never affect population."],"answer":"The sources corroborate growth while contributing different kinds of evidence about its causes and effects.","explain":"The sources corroborate growth while contributing different kinds of evidence about its causes and effects. This inference connects the named evidence to its context while avoiding an unsupported absolute conclusion.","audio":"A map shows neighborhood growth, a newspaper explains a new road, and interviews describe residents' experiences. Which inference remains most defensible after checking chronology and context?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Evidence A: An early settlement map clusters homes near a river crossing and fertile land. Evidence B: A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?","choices":["Evidence A proves “Communities form without any relationship to people or place.,” so Evidence B can be ignored.","Both sources prove that one event caused every later development, regardless of chronology or context.","Because the sources discuss related topics, neither source needs corroboration.","Evidence A supports “Communities often develop where people can obtain water, transportation, work, or other resources.” Evidence B supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context."],"answer":"Evidence A supports “Communities often develop where people can obtain water, transportation, work, or other resources.” Evidence B supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","explain":"Evidence A supports “Communities often develop where people can obtain water, transportation, work, or other resources.” Evidence B supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context. This response states what each source supports and avoids turning related evidence into proof of a single, universal cause.","audio":"Evidence A: An early settlement map clusters homes near a river crossing and fertile land. Evidence B: A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?"},
      {"type":"mc","q":"Evidence A: A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Evidence B: Volunteer records show residents organizing food distribution after a flood. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?","choices":["Both sources prove that one event caused every later development, regardless of chronology or context.","Because the sources discuss related topics, neither source needs corroboration.","Evidence A supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Evidence B supports “People contribute through work, service, leadership, and participation.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","Evidence A proves “A community is defined only by the number of houses it contains.,” so Evidence B can be ignored."],"answer":"Evidence A supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Evidence B supports “People contribute through work, service, leadership, and participation.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context.","explain":"Evidence A supports “Schools, markets, religious centers, and public spaces help organize community life.” Evidence B supports “People contribute through work, service, leadership, and participation.” Together, the sources show related developments while preserving each source's context. This response states what each source supports and avoids turning related evidence into proof of a single, universal cause.","audio":"Evidence A: A town directory shows a school, market, courthouse, and several places of worship around a central square. Evidence B: Volunteer records show residents organizing food distribution after a flood. Which thesis best evaluates both sources without overstating either one?"}
